.SH NAME
messq-auth-ls - list token ids, roles and stream patterns — never hashes


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBmessq auth ls [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
List every token in the auth-file with its id, role set and stream patterns.
The digest column is deliberately absent: someone holding only a stored
hash could replay requests offline against any endpoint that accepts the
presented form, so listings prove nothing beyond WHAT each id may do.
Editing grants means editing the file line; the daemon picks the change up
when the reload seam lands with issue #17.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB--auth-file\fP=""
	token file to inspect (or MESSQ_AUTH_FILE)

.PP
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for ls


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB-a\fP, \fB--addr\fP="unix:///run/messq/messq.sock"
	daemon address (unix://, http://, https://)

.PP
\fB--color\fP="auto"
	colour: auto|always|never

.PP
\fB--full-ids\fP[=false]
	print full ULIDs instead of abbreviated ones

.PP
\fB-o\fP, \fB--output\fP="auto"
	output mode: auto|table|json|ndjson

.PP
\fB-q\fP, \fB--quiet\fP[=false]
	suppress narration; data and errors unaffected

.PP
\fB--timeout\fP=30s
	per-request deadline (0 = none)

.PP
\fB--token-file\fP=""
	file holding the bearer token, mode 0600

.PP
\fB-v\fP, \fB--verbose\fP[=0]
	narrate (-vv dumps each HTTP exchange and the resolved config)


.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
  messq auth ls --auth-file /etc/messq/tokens
  messq auth ls --output json | jq '.tokens[].id'
.EE


